{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"randomSpanningTree","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"using","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"generator","kind":"internalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"inout","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"some","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"RandomNumberGenerator","preciseIdentifier":"s:SG","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":") -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"SpanningForest","identifier":"doc:\/\/SpanningTrees\/documentation\/SpanningTrees\/SpanningForest","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:13SpanningTrees0A6ForestV"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"parameters":[{"name":"generator","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The source of randomness."}]}]}],"kind":"parameters"},{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"return-value","text":"Return Value","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The forest’s edges, in the order they joined, and its weight, the number of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edges.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each component is rooted at its first vertex in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" order. Every other vertex, in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that order, starts a random walk that stops on reaching the tree; the walk with its loops","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"erased joins the tree. The result is uniform over the spanning forests that have one tree","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"per component, each copy of a parallel edge counting as its own edge. Self-loops are never","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"taken. The forest depends only on the generator’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"next()"},{"text":" sequence, so a seeded generator","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"reproduces it on every platform.","type":"text"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(m) plus the walks: the mean hitting time of the root, expected, which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"O(n m) at worst and far less on well-connected graphs."}]}],"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Complexity"}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A spanning forest drawn uniformly at random, by Wilson’s algorithm: one uniform spanning"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tree per connected component."}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/spanningtrees\/graphprotocols\/graph\/randomspanningtree(using:)"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SpanningTrees\/documentation\/SpanningTrees","doc:\/\/SpanningTrees\/documentation\/SpanningTrees\/GraphProtocols","doc:\/\/SpanningTrees\/documentation\/SpanningTrees\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"]]},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"SpanningTrees","relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"]}],"roleHeading":"Instance Method","symbolKind":"method","externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P13SpanningTreesE06randomC4Tree5usingAD0C6ForestVyxSiGqd__z_tSGRd__lF","extendedModule":"GraphProtocols","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"randomSpanningTree"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"kind":"externalParam","text":"using"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"inout"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"ki